{"text": "ILLINOIS JOURNAL OF MATHEMATICS\nVolume 38, Number 3, Fall 1994\n\nKNOTS AND SHELLABLE CELL PARTITIONINGS OF S?\u00b0\n\nSTEVE ARMENTROUT\n\nA cell partitioning of S\u00b0 is a finite covering H of S* by 3-cells such that if\nm is any positive integer and exactly m 3-cells of H intersect, their common\npart is a cell of dimension 4 \u2014 m, where cells of negative dimension are\nempty. The 3-cells of a cell partitioning of S* fit together in a staggered,\nbrick-like pattern.\nA cell partitioning H of S* is shellable if and only if there is a counting\n(h,,h2,\u00b0*+,h,) of H such that if i is an integer and 1<i<n, then\nh, Uh, U ++: Uh; is a 3-cell. Such a counting is a shelling of H.\nIn this paper, we shall study a connection between knots in S* and\nshellability of cell partitionings of 5\u00b0. We shall use these results to construct\nnonshellable cell partitionings of S>.\nOur results involve the use of the bridge number of a knot in S*. In\nSection 1 of this paper, we shall review some results concerning knots in S?\nand bridge numbers of knots in 5%. In Section 2, we shall establish the main\nresult of the paper. In Section 3, we shall establish a variant of the main\nresult that is useful in some situations. In Section 4, we shall use the results\nof this paper to construct a nonshellable cell partitioning of S* and, as a\nvariation on that construction, a nest of nonshellable cell partitionings of S>.\nThroughout this paper, we shall assume that S* has its standard piecewise\nlinear structure.\nThe author thanks the referee for suggestions and corrections.\n\n1. Knots in S?\n\nA knot in S\u00b0 is a polygonal simple closed curve in S*. Two knots k and /\nin S? are of the same knot type in S\u00b0 if and only if there is an orientation\npreserving PL homeomorphism f: S* > S? such that f(k) = /. A knot in S$?\nis trivial if and only if it has the same knot type as the boundary of a\n2-simplex in S\u00b0.\nSuppose C is a 3-cell. Then @ is a spanning arc of C if and only if @ is an\narc in C such that Bd a C BdC and Int a@ C Int C. D is a semispanning disc\nof C if and only if D is a disc in C such that Int D C Int C and DN BdC is\nan arc on Bd C. The statement that B is a straight spanning arc of C means\nthat B is a spanning arc of C and there is a semispanning disc D of C such\nthat B C Bd D. Recall that if B is a polyhedral straight spanning arc of a\n\nReceived February 28, 1991.\n1991 Mathematics Subject Classification. Primary 57M50; Secondary 57M25.\n\n\u00a9 1994 by the Board of Trustees of the University of Illinois\nManufactured in the United States of America\n\n347",
